in this video you're going to learn how  to find the area of a segment  in a circle so what exactly is the area  of a segment in a circle  well see when we think of a segment  we're usually thinking of a line segment  right  but what it is is this little kind of  half moon shape right here that i've  shaded in so what you have is you have a  sector  which is basically like a pie slice you  know like a slice of pie  but then what you're doing is you're  subtracting off this triangle here  and you're left with just this area of  this like i said like a moon shape  so in order to find the area of this  segment here what we have to do is we  have to use a couple different formulas  we have to find the area of the sector  okay that's the pi slice that i  mentioned right here and then we have to  subtract off the  triangle so that we're left with this  remaining half moon  quantity here which is we refer to as a  segment and in this first example i'm  going to do it using the  formula one half a b sine c and what  this refers to it's just the area of a  triangle but you're taking  the side times the side  times the sine of the included angle the  angle in between those two sides  times one half and that's a quick way of  getting the area of that triangle  so let's do that method first so in this  particular example we have a central  angle of 150 degrees  so to find the area of this sector we're  going to say  150 divided by 360  times pi r squared in this case the  radius is 4.  now if you notice the pi r squared  that's the area of the whole circle  theta divided by 360 or in this case 150  divided by 360 is the  fraction of the whole circle so for  example if we were to reduce this down  by dividing by  10 and if we divide by 3 top and bottom  that gives us  5 12. so this really represents 5 12  of the area of the whole circle 16 pi  so let's go ahead and simplify that down  a little bit further  and i'm just going to use 3.14 for pi or  i'm just going to use the pi key so  we're going to say  5 times pi times 4 squared which is 16  divided by 12  is going to come out to 20.94 i'm just  rounding a little bit here  so 20.94 is the area of our sector  minus now the area of our triangle we're  going to use this formula here  one half now a you see is four  b here is also four since the radius is  always four  so we have one half four times four  times the sine of the included angle  which is 150 degrees  when you're doing this make sure you're  in the degree mode on your calculator so  let's see one half  times 4 times 4 which is 16 times the  sine of 150  it comes out to 4. so you can see 20.94  minus 4  is 16.94  inches squared that's the area of the  segment  now you're saying mario what about  what's the second method well the second  method  is what you can do to find the area of  this triangle  is you can drop a perpendicular like so  okay now see how this is an isosceles  triangle  so what's going to happen here is this  is a 150 degrees we're actually  bisecting that angle so it's going to be  75 degrees  75 degrees and then what you can do is  you can do some trigonometry okay sine  and cosine  and you can find out what's the height  of this triangle which is right here  what's this length here and you can  double it to get the base and then you  can do one half base times height to  find the area of that triangle so you  want to see that part okay so i'll show  you that part  so i'm just going to call this piece  right here h okay  so let's draw that triangle a little bit  bigger so we can see it so it looks  something like this so  this is going to be h and this i'm going  to call this just  x so we have 75 degrees the hypotenuse  here is  4 and what trig function ties together  adjacent and hypotenuse  that's cosine so we have the cosine of  75 degrees  is equal to h over 4 and what trig  function  ties together opposite in hypotenuse  that's the sine so we have sine of 75  degrees  equals x over 4. and now if i just  multiply both sides by  4 that gives us h and if i multiply here  both sides by 4 that gives us  x so let me go ahead and do that on the  calculator we've got 4 cosine 75  which is about 1.035  okay going around too much too early and  we've got 4 times the sine of 75  which is 3.864  and that's x now the thing is see how we  got 3.864 here  this piece here is also 3.864 so we have  to add those together or multiply by 2.  so that comes out to about 7.727 okay  so now the area of our triangle we know  the old way of finding the area of a  triangle one-half base times height  we've grown up with that  the base here is going to be 7.727  the height we said was going to be 1.035  and if we multiply all that together  just make sure we're coming out to four  okay i'm rounding a little bit here and  you can see that's the same area we got  when we use the one half a b  sine c but the whole key here is it's  kind of like a two part  a problem you want to find the area of  sector minus area of the triangle  equals the area of the segment if you  want to see another example in another  video that i did talking about  this concept here about finding the area  of a segment follow me over that video  right there  and i'll see you in that video
 

